.TH SNATCHER 1 2026-09-12 snatcher "Snatcher Manual"
.SH NAME
snatcher \- Transmission BitTorrent CLI client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snatcher
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ "command options" ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B snatcher
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.br
.B snatcher
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snatcher
is a modern CLI and TUI for managing the Transmission BitTorrent client.
It is built so that anything the TUI can do is also scriptable from the command
line.
.PP
Run
.B snatcher
with no arguments on a terminal to start the TUI. A subcommand,
.BR \-\-help ,
or a redirected standard input or output keeps the command\-line behavior, so
.B snatcher | cat
prints usage rather than trying to draw a full\-screen interface. The TUI is
currently a shell: it lays out the torrents and settings windows, routes focus
between panes, and renders placeholder data. Live server data arrives in a
later release.
.PP
snatcher requires
.B Transmission 4.0 or newer
(RPC version 17 or higher) and refuses to operate against older servers,
reporting an unsupported\-version error instead of failing in confusing ways.
.PP
Commands speak to a single configured Transmission server over its RPC
interface. The non-secret connection settings (server URL and username) are
stored in a JSON config file; the password is kept in a secret store, by
default the operating system keyring (see
.BR SECRETS ).
Run
.B snatcher server add
once to configure a server before using the other commands.
.PP
One command group,
.BR "snatcher search" ,
talks to
.B Prowlarr
rather than to Transmission. It searches the indexers a Prowlarr instance is
configured with, lists those indexers and the torznab categories they use,
hands a release back to Prowlarr to grab, and writes a release's
.B .torrent
file to this machine. A Prowlarr instance is registered as a server alias of
its own with
.BR "snatcher server add \-\-type prowlarr" ,
and its API key lives in the same secret store a Transmission password does.
Searching finds a release; putting one on
.I this
server is still
.BR "snatcher torrent add" .
.PP
Every command that produces output renders in one of three modes with a fixed
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " \(-> " \-\-json " \(-> plaintext."
The JSON field names are a stable scripting contract. Human and plaintext
output is sanitized of terminal control sequences (server-derived names,
labels, errors, and paths are untrusted); JSON and jq output is never
sanitized, since it is a structured contract the consumer handles.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global options
These are declared once, on
.B snatcher
itself, and are accepted by every command at every depth \(em
.B snatcher \-\-config
.I path
.B torrent list
and
.B snatcher torrent list \-\-config
.I path
are the same invocation.
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Read configuration from
.I path
instead of the default location (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-\-theme " " \fIname\fR
Use the color theme
.IR name ,
one of
.BR default ", " dracula ", " nord ", or " solarized ,
for this invocation only, overriding the
.B client.theme
setting. An unknown name is an error listing the valid ones.
.IP
.B default
is the terminal's own sixteen colors, so it follows whatever color scheme the
terminal is configured with;
.BR dracula ", " nord ", and " solarized
are fixed palettes, each with a light and a dark arrangement chosen from the
terminal's background color.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-nerd\-font
Draw ASCII icons rather than Nerd Font glyphs for this invocation only,
overriding the
.B client.nerd_font
setting. The flag is negative and the setting is positive, so
.B \-\-no\-nerd\-font
means
.BR client.nerd_font=false ;
.B \-\-no\-nerd\-font=false
asks for glyphs explicitly.
.IP
Both options are the TUI's: they choose the palette it draws with and whether
it may use Nerd Font glyphs, and no other command's output changes. Their full
precedence chain is
.BR \-\-theme / \-\-no\-nerd\-font
over
.BR SNATCHER_THEME / SNATCHER_NO_NERD_FONT
over the config file, over the built-in default;
.B snatcher config list
reports which rung supplied each value. An invalid value at any rung is an
error naming that rung, never a silent fallback, on every command that reads
the setting \(em the TUI,
.BR "config list" ", and " "config get" .
A command that reads neither is unaffected, so a stale
.B SNATCHER_THEME
cannot fail the
.B config
commands that would repair it.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit. Valid on
.B snatcher
itself.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help for
.B snatcher
or any subcommand and exit.
.SS Common options
These options are accepted by the
.BR server ,
.BR torrent ,
.BR search ,
and
.B config
subcommands. They do not all apply everywhere: the named-server registry
subcommands
.RB ( server\ add ", " server\ list ", " server\ default ", " server\ show ", " server\ ping ", " server\ remove )
read or write local config and pick a server by positional alias, so they take
.BR \-\-json / \-\-jq
where they emit data but never
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B config
subcommands never contact a server at all, so they take
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
but never
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B api
command accepts
.B \-\-jq
but uses
.B \-\-raw
in place of
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-\-server " " \fIalias\fR
Target the configured server named
.IR alias
instead of the default. When omitted, snatcher uses the
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
environment variable if set, otherwise the configured default server. This
option is accepted only by commands that connect to a server; it never appears
on the named-server registry subcommands
.RB ( server\ add ", " server\ list ", " server\ default ", " server\ ping ", " server\ remove ),
.BR version ,
or
.BR completion .
It is also absent from
.BR "snatcher server show" ,
which names the server it reports on with a positional
.I alias
instead. On
.B snatcher search
\(em and on
.B "snatcher api"
with
.B \-\-prowlarr
\(em the alias must name a
.B prowlarr
server rather than a Transmission one, and an omitted alias falls back to the
configured default Prowlarr server.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of plaintext.
.TP
.BR \-\-jq " " \fIexpr\fR
Filter JSON output through the jq expression
.IR expr .
Implies JSON and takes precedence over
.BR \-\-json .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "server"
Manage configured servers and the server itself. The
.B server
group maintains an inventory of named servers \(em the registry:
.BR add ", " list ", " default ", " ping ", " remove
\(em and operates on the selected server: its session settings, statistics,
BitTorrent listening port, global speed limits, and download-path free space
.RB ( show ", " update ", " reset ", " stats ", " port ", " bandwidth ", " free-space ).
.TP
.B snatcher server add \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Add or update a named server. Its URL, type, and username are stored in the
config file under the server's alias and its secret in a secret store, by
default the OS keyring. Two kinds of server can be registered, chosen with
.BR \-\-type :
a Transmission server, which authenticates with a password, and a Prowlarr
instance, which authenticates with an API key. The password is read from a
hidden, non-echoing prompt (with confirmation) or, for scripting, from standard
input with
.BR \-\-password\-stdin ;
an API key is read from a hidden prompt asked
.I once
\(em an API key is pasted, not typed, so a confirmation step catches nothing \(em
or from standard input with
.BR \-\-api\-key\-stdin .
An empty password is a valid choice (a server with authentication disabled)
and stores no secret; an empty API key is accepted too, with a note on standard
error that search commands will fail unless
.B SNATCHER_API_KEY
is set. When the alias's URL or its secret store changes, the
secret it used to keep is deleted from the store that held it, so no password
is left behind in a store the server no longer uses. The first server of each
type registered when that type has no default becomes it \(em the Transmission
default and the Prowlarr default are separate, so registering one never
repoints the other. Re-adding an existing
alias with a different type is refused unless
.B \-\-type
is passed explicitly, so an omitted flag cannot silently turn a registered
Prowlarr instance into a Transmission server and strand its API key. Options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-name " " \fIalias\fR
Alias the server is stored under. When omitted it defaults to the lowercased
URL host (including the port).
.TP
.BR \-\-url " " \fIurl\fR
Server URL: a Transmission RPC URL, e.g.\&
.IR http://host:9091/transmission/rpc ,
or a Prowlarr base URL, e.g.\&
.IR http://host:9696 .
Required. Must be an absolute http or https URL with no embedded credentials,
whichever type is being registered.
.TP
.BR \-\-type " " \fIname\fR
Kind of server this alias names, one of
.BR transmission " (the default) or " prowlarr ,
matched case-insensitively. A Transmission server records no type at all, so a
config file written before the option existed round-trips unchanged. An
unrecognized value is an error naming the valid ones, never a silent fallback.
.TP
.BR \-\-username " " \fIname\fR
Username for HTTP Basic Auth. Transmission only; it is rejected with
.BR "\-\-type prowlarr" ,
which authenticates with an API key and no user.
.TP
.B \-\-password\-stdin
Read the password from standard input instead of prompting. Transmission only;
it is rejected with
.BR "\-\-type prowlarr" .
.TP
.B \-\-api\-key\-stdin
Read the Prowlarr API key from standard input instead of prompting. Prowlarr
only; it is rejected without
.BR "\-\-type prowlarr" .
.TP
.BR \-\-store " " \fIname\fR
Secret store this server's secret \(em its password, or its Prowlarr API key
\(em lives in, one of
.BR keyring ", " file ", or " none .
.B keyring
is the default: the operating system keyring, and only the keyring \(em it
never falls back to another store when it cannot be reached.
.B file
writes the password in
.B plaintext
to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json ,
created with mode 0600 under a 0700 directory; it exists for machines with no
OS keyring and is less safe than the keyring.
.B none
stores nothing at all. Whichever store is chosen is both written to and saved
with the server, so later commands use the same store without repeating the
option. When the option is omitted the store is resolved the usual way \(em
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
first, then the store the alias already had, then
.B keyring
\(em and that result is saved. An explicit
.B \-\-store
outranks
.BR SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE ,
which is the one place it does; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List every configured server, sorted by alias, showing its name, type, URL,
username, whether a secret is stored, and whether it is the default. The columns
are
.BR NAME " " TYPE " " URL " " USERNAME " " SECRET " " DEFAULT .
.B TYPE
is
.BR transmission " or " prowlarr ;
a server registered before the type existed reports
.BR transmission ,
never an empty column. Because each type has its own default, the
.B DEFAULT
column is true when the alias is the default
.I for its own type
\(em a Transmission server and a Prowlarr instance can both be marked default
at once. Secret status is
read from the store each server itself names
.RB ( secret_store ),
not the one
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
selects for this invocation, so an exported override cannot report a stored
password as missing. Plaintext is
an aligned table on a terminal and tab-separated, header-less rows when piped or
when
.B NO_COLOR
is set (the same contract as
.BR list ).
Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server default \fIalias\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Set the default server to the existing alias
.IR alias .
Which default it sets is inferred from the alias, with no flag: a Transmission
server becomes
.BR default_server ,
a Prowlarr instance becomes
.BR default_prowlarr_server ,
and setting one never repoints the other. The confirmation line names which of
the two was set
.RB ( "Default server set to \(dqx\(dq" " or " "Default Prowlarr server set to \(dqx\(dq" ).
The default is used by every command that connects to a server of that type when
.B \-\-server
and
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
are not given. An unknown alias is rejected, listing the configured names.
It takes no options of its own.
.TP
.B snatcher server show \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show everything about a server: its local connection settings (name, URL,
username, resolved config path, whether a secret is stored, whether it is
the default), its live identity (version, RPC semver, negotiated protocol), and
every session setting. Shows the named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. The secret value itself is
never revealed, and whether one is stored is read from the store the server
itself names, as in
.BR "snatcher server list" .
.IP
Identity and settings are two projections of one
.B session\-get
response, so the whole view costs a single round-trip. A first run with no
servers configured reports the empty connection block rather than an error, and
never dials.
.IP
Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
connection block, a blank line, then the settings as a
.BR CATEGORY " " KEY " " VALUE
table (the same terminal/piped contract as
.BR list ).
When piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set, the connection block becomes header-less
.RI key <TAB> value
lines using kebab-case keys, followed directly by the tab-separated settings
rows. Speed limits
.RB ( speed\-limit\-down ", " speed\-limit\-up ", " alt\-speed\-down ", " alt\-speed\-up )
are in KB/s; the alt-speed schedule times
.RB ( alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end )
are minutes after midnight (e.g.\& 540 is 09:00). Use
.B \-\-jq
to read a single value in a script, e.g.\&
.BR "port=$(snatcher server show --jq '.settings[]|select(.key==\(dqpeer-port\(dq).value')" .
Besides
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq ,
it accepts:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-local
Show only the local connection settings, without contacting the server. Cannot
be combined with
.BR \-\-category ", " \-\-search ", or " \-\-fields .
.TP
.BR \-\-category " " \fIname\fR
Show only settings in one category. One of
.BR download ", " network ", " bandwidth ", " queue .
Applies to every output mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only settings whose key contains
.I text
(case-insensitive). Applies to every output mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated keys to show, naming connection fields
.RB ( name ", " server\-url ", " username ", " config\-path ", " secret\-stored ", "
.BR is\-default ", " version ", " rpc\-version\-semver ", " protocol ),
setting keys, or both. It selects without reordering \(em each section keeps
its canonical order \(em has no effect on
.B \-\-json
(always the full object) or
.B \-\-jq
(which selects fields itself), and cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-category " or " \-\-search .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server ping \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Dial a server and perform a real round-trip, reporting what it found. Pings the
named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. Exits non-zero on failure.
Accepts
.B \-\-json .
.IP
The round-trip depends on the resolved server's type. A Transmission server
reports its connection status, type, version, negotiated protocol, and RPC
semver; a Prowlarr instance is asked for its system status and reports its
connection status, type, version, and application name. An omitted alias falls
back to the
.I Transmission
default, which is what every invocation resolved to before Prowlarr servers
could be registered; ping a Prowlarr instance by naming it, or through
.BR SNATCHER_SERVER .
.TP
.B snatcher server remove \fR[\fIalias\fR] \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Remove a configured server: delete its stored secret, drop its entry from the
config file, and persist. Removes the named server
.IR alias ,
or the configured default when the alias is omitted. The secret is deleted from
the store the server itself names
.RB ( secret_store ),
not the one
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
selects for this invocation, so an exported override cannot leave a password
behind in a store nothing points at any more. When the removed server was
the default, only the default belonging to
.I its own type
moves: it is repointed to the sole remaining server of that type if exactly one
remains, otherwise it is cleared. Removing a Prowlarr instance therefore never
touches
.BR default_server ,
and no default is ever repointed at a server of the other type. An unknown alias
is rejected. It takes no options of its own.
.TP
.B snatcher server stats \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show session statistics: torrent counts, current download and upload speeds,
and cumulative and current-session transfer totals. Accepts the common options
only.
.TP
.B snatcher server update \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Change one or more server (session) settings, each given as a typed flag whose
name is the setting key (e.g.\&
.BR \-\-peer\-port " " 51413 " " \-\-dht\-enabled = false ).
Only the flags you pass are written, so unspecified settings are left
unchanged; at least one setting flag is required (a bare invocation is an error,
not a no-op). Bool settings are enabled with a bare
.B \-\-key
and disabled with
.BR \-\-key = false .
Enum flags are validated before any network call, so an invalid value aborts
with no partial change. After writing, the values you set are echoed back \(em
this is what was written, not a re-read, so server-side normalization (e.g.\&
clamping an out-of-range port) is not reflected. Speed limits
.RB ( speed\-limit\-down ", " speed\-limit\-up ", " alt\-speed\-down ", " alt\-speed\-up )
are in KB/s; the alt-speed schedule times
.RB ( alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end )
are minutes after midnight (e.g.\& 540 is 09:00). Read the settings back with
.BR "snatcher server show" ,
and restore defaults with
.BR "snatcher server reset" .
The read-only
.B blocklist\-size
has no flag. Besides the common
.BR \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq
options, one flag exists per settable setting. Download:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Default directory for completed downloads.
.TP
.BR \-\-start\-added\-torrents [= true | false ]
Start torrents automatically when added.
.TP
.BR \-\-rename\-partial\-files [= true | false ]
Append
.B .part
to incomplete files.
.TP
.BR \-\-incomplete\-dir\-enabled [= true | false ]
Keep incomplete downloads in a separate directory.
.TP
.BR \-\-incomplete\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Directory for incomplete downloads (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio\-limited [= true | false ]
Stop seeding at the global seed-ratio limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio\-limit " " \fIratio\fR
Global seed ratio to stop seeding at (when limited).
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-seeding\-limit\-enabled [= true | false ]
Stop seeding torrents that have been idle too long.
.TP
.BR \-\-idle\-seeding\-limit " " \fIminutes\fR
Idle time in minutes before stopping seeding (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-cache\-size\-mib " " \fIn\fR
Disk cache size in MiB.
.RE
.IP
Network:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-port " " \fIn\fR
Port to listen for incoming peer connections.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-port\-random\-on\-start [= true | false ]
Pick a random peer port on each start.
.TP
.BR \-\-port\-forwarding\-enabled [= true | false ]
Request UPnP/NAT-PMP port forwarding.
.TP
.BR \-\-encryption " " \fIallowed | preferred | required\fR
Peer encryption mode.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit\-global " " \fIn\fR
Maximum peer connections across all torrents.
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit\-per\-torrent " " \fIn\fR
Maximum peer connections per torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-pex\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable Peer Exchange (PEX).
.TP
.BR \-\-dht\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the Distributed Hash Table (DHT).
.TP
.BR \-\-lpd\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable Local Peer Discovery (LPD).
.TP
.BR \-\-utp\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the uTP transport protocol.
.TP
.BR \-\-blocklist\-enabled [= true | false ]
Block peers listed in the blocklist.
.TP
.BR \-\-blocklist\-url " " \fIurl\fR
URL to fetch the peer blocklist from.
.RE
.IP
Bandwidth (the speed limits are in KB/s; the schedule times are minutes after
midnight):
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-down\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the global download speed limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-down " " \fIKBps\fR
Global download speed limit in KB/s (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-up\-enabled [= true | false ]
Enable the global upload speed limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-speed\-limit\-up " " \fIKBps\fR
Global upload speed limit in KB/s (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-enabled [= true | false ]
Use the alternative (throttled) speed limits now.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-down " " \fIKBps\fR
Alternative download speed limit in KB/s.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-up " " \fIKBps\fR
Alternative upload speed limit in KB/s.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-enabled [= true | false ]
Switch to alternative speeds on a daily schedule.
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-begin " " \fIminutes\fR
Alternative-speed schedule start (minutes after midnight).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-end " " \fIminutes\fR
Alternative-speed schedule end (minutes after midnight).
.TP
.BR \-\-alt\-speed\-time\-day " " \fIn\fR
Days the alternative-speed schedule runs (bitfield, Sun=1).
.RE
.IP
Queue:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-queue\-enabled [= true | false ]
Limit how many torrents download at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-queue\-size " " \fIn\fR
Maximum torrents downloading at once (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-queue\-enabled [= true | false ]
Limit how many torrents seed at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-queue\-size " " \fIn\fR
Maximum torrents seeding at once (when enabled).
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-stalled\-enabled [= true | false ]
Ignore stalled torrents when counting the queue.
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-stalled\-minutes " " \fIn\fR
Idle minutes before a transfer counts as stalled.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher server reset \fIkey\fR...
Restore one or more settings to their Transmission defaults. Every key is
validated before anything is sent, so an unknown or read-only key (the only
read-only setting is
.BR blocklist\-size )
aborts the whole batch with no partial change; passing no key is an error, not
a no-op. Every default travels in one write, after which the settings are
re-read and the server's confirmed values are reported \(em the same output
shape as
.BR "snatcher server update" ,
in registry order regardless of the order the keys were named. Accepts
.BR \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
View, change, and test the BitTorrent peer/listening port \(em a discoverable
wrapper over the peer-port session settings. With no subcommand it prints help.
.TP
.B snatcher server port get \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Show the current peer port alongside the random-on-start and port-forwarding
flags read from the live session. Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
block;
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { port ", " random_on_start ", " forwarding_enabled }
object. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port set \fR[\fIport\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Set the peer port and/or related flags, then re-read the session and report the
resulting state in the same shape as
.BR "server port get" .
A
.I port
must be an integer in
.RB [ 1 ", " 65535 ];
giving one also disables
.B random_on_start
so the port sticks across restarts, unless
.B \-\-random
is passed explicitly. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-random\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Randomize the peer port on each server start. A bare
.B \-\-random
means true; an explicit flag overrides the auto-disable triggered by a
.I port
argument.
.TP
.B \-\-forwarding\fR[=\fIbool\fR]
Enable or disable port forwarding (UPnP/NAT-PMP).
.RE
.IP
With neither a
.I port
nor any flag, it returns a "nothing to set" error. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server port test \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Test whether the currently configured peer port is reachable from outside (it
takes no
.I port
argument). Reports
.B open
or
.B closed
in plaintext and emits an
.RB { open ", " ip_protocol }
object under
.BR \-\-json
(\c
.B ip_protocol
is reported only by Transmission's JSON-RPC 2.0 scheme and omitted otherwise).
Exits 0 when the port is open and non-zero when it is closed, while still
writing the structured result to stdout; a connection or authentication failure
is a distinct non-zero exit that renders no result. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server bandwidth \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
View and change the global download and upload speed limits, including the
alt (turtle) profile \(em an ergonomic wrapper over the same session settings
.B server update
exposes one raw key at a time, so a limit and its enabled companion never have
to be written by hand. With no subcommand it prints help.
.TP
.B snatcher server bandwidth show \fR[\fIdownload\fR|\fIupload\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Show the current transfer rates, both global limit pairs, and whether the alt
(turtle) profile is active. With no argument both directions are reported;
naming
.B download
or
.B upload
(case-insensitively) reports only that one, and the other direction is absent
from the output entirely. Plaintext is an aligned block; the alt state and its
schedule state are always reported, whatever the narrowing. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-alt
Report the alt (turtle) limit for each direction instead of the normal-profile
limit and its enabled flag.
.RE
.IP
Current speeds are in bytes per second and render as human-readable rates (e.g.
.BR "1.5 MB/s" ),
while every limit is an integer in
.B KB/s
exactly as Transmission stores it \(em the two units are never converted into
one another. A limit whose enabled flag is false still shows its stored value,
marked
.BR (disabled) ,
because turning a limit off preserves the number.
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { download ", " upload ", " alt_enabled ", " alt_schedule_enabled }
object whose direction values carry
.BR current_speed ", " limit ", " limit_enabled ", and " alt_limit ;
the object always contains exactly the fields the plaintext block shows.
Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server bandwidth update \fIdownload\fR|\fIupload\fR \fIspeed\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Set the global speed limit for one direction. The direction is
.B download
or
.B upload
(case-insensitively). The
.I speed
is a non-negative integer in
.BR KB/s ,
exactly as Transmission stores it \(em suffixed values such as
.B 5M
are not accepted \(em or the literal
.B off
(case-insensitively). Setting a limit also turns it on, writing the value and
its enabled companion in a single call;
.B off
turns the limit off while leaving the stored value in place, so re-enabling
restores it. A
.I speed
of
.B 0
is a real 0 KB/s limit \(em traffic in that direction stops \(em and does not
mean unlimited; use
.B off
to remove a limit. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-alt
Write the alt (turtle) profile's limit for that direction instead of the
normal-profile limit.
.RE
.IP
The alt profile has a single switch governing both directions, so an
.B \-\-alt
write never touches it and
.B off
is rejected with an error \(em use
.B snatcher server bandwidth alt off
to leave turtle mode. An alt limit therefore has no per-direction
.BR off :
a
.I speed
of
.B 0
under
.B \-\-alt
writes a real 0 KB/s alt limit that halts that direction whenever turtle mode
is active, and
.B snatcher server bandwidth alt off
leaves turtle mode rather than clearing the limit.
After writing, the server's actual values are read back
and reported in the same shape as
.BR "snatcher server bandwidth show" ,
narrowed to the direction and profile that was written. A negative
.I speed
must follow a
.B \-\-
separator so it is not read as a flag. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server bandwidth alt \fIon\fR|\fIoff\fR|\fItoggle\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Turn the alt (turtle) speed profile on or off, or toggle it. The state is
.BR on ", " off ", or " toggle
(case-insensitively); anything else is an error and no request is sent. This is
the one switch that decides whether the alt limits are in force, and it governs
both directions at once, which is why an
.B \-\-alt
write in
.B snatcher server bandwidth update
never touches it. The subcommand takes no
.B \-\-alt
flag of its own.
.B on
and
.B off
write the switch directly without reading it first;
.B toggle
reads the current state and writes its inverse, and because Transmission's RPC
offers no compare-and-set, another client flipping the switch between that read
and the write wins. A configured alt-speed schedule is the likelier surprise:
with one in place the server flips this switch itself at the schedule
boundaries, so a manual
.B on
or
.B off
holds only until the next boundary \(em no second client involved.
.B snatcher server bandwidth show
reports whether a schedule is configured, as
.B alt_schedule_enabled
in
.B \-\-json
and as
.B (schedule: yes)
on the plaintext
.B Alt\-speed
line. The window itself \(em
.BR alt\-speed\-time\-begin ", " alt\-speed\-time\-end ", and"
.B alt\-speed\-time\-day
\(em is shown by
.BR "snatcher server show \-\-category bandwidth" .
After the write the complete state is read back and
reported unnarrowed \(em both directions and every limit field \(em in the same
shape as
.BR "snatcher server bandwidth show" ,
since flipping the switch changes which limits apply everywhere. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher server free-space \fR[\fIpath\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
Show the free space on a download path. With no
.I path
it queries the server's default download directory. Plaintext is an aligned
.RI Label : " value"
block;
.B \-\-json
emits a stable
.RB { path ", " size_bytes ", " total_bytes }
object. The
.B total_bytes
total capacity is reported only by Transmission 4.1+ and omitted otherwise (both
in the object and the plaintext block). Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.SS "torrent"
Manage individual torrents. All torrent operations live under the
.B torrent
command group:
.BR add ", " show ", " files ", " peers ", " trackers ", " list ", " start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " move ", " rename ", " queue ", " remove ", " update ", and " label .
The mutation subcommands
.RB ( start ", " stop ", " verify ", " reannounce ", " remove ", " update ),
and every
.B label
subcommand, select torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare invocation is an error, not a
no-op. Every subcommand accepts the common options.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent add \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile | url | magnet\fR]...
Add torrents to the server from local
.I .torrent
files, HTTP(S) URLs, or magnet links. Each argument is auto-detected as a
file, URL, or magnet link. With no positional arguments, sources are read from
standard input, one per line; a bare
.B snatcher torrent add
with no sources and no piped input is an error. Multiple sources are processed
continue-on-error: a failure adding one source is reported but does not stop
the rest, and the command exits non-zero if any source failed. Besides the
common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-start
Start torrents immediately instead of adding them paused.
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-dir " " \fIpath\fR
Server-side destination directory.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delete
Delete the local
.I .torrent
after a successful, non-duplicate file add.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIlabel\fR
Label to apply to added torrents. May be repeated.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress per-source confirmation lines (plaintext only; never alters JSON/jq
output).
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent show \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
.RB ( info )
Show the full set of fields for a single torrent, identified by its numeric id
or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required. On a terminal
the output is an aligned
.IR key ": " value
block; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is
.IR key <TAB> value
lines with no header \(em a stable scripting contract. When
.B \-\-fields
selects exactly one field the piped form drops the key column too, printing
the bare value on its own line so it can be captured verbatim. With
.B \-\-json
the torrent is emitted as a single JSON object (not an array), and
.B \-\-jq
filters that object. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated fields to show in the plaintext output, in the given order.
When omitted every field except
.B magnet_link
is shown. Available fields:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels ", " comment ", " creator ", " date_created ,
.BR is_private ", " piece_count ", " piece_size ", " downloaded_ever ,
.BR uploaded_ever ", " corrupt_ever ", " have_valid ,
.BR seconds_downloading ", " seconds_seeding ", " recheck_progress ,
.BR metadata_percent_complete ", " magnet_link .
.IP
.B magnet_link
is opt-in: it is selectable but excluded from the default set, because a magnet
URI is a single long line that would dominate the block. Select it on its own
to print just the magnet URI \(em no label, no tab \(em e.g.
.IP
.EX
snatcher torrent show \-\-fields magnet_link 3 | pbcopy
.EE
.IP
This affects plaintext only:
.B \-\-json
always emits the full object and
.B \-\-jq
selects fields itself.
.IP
A magnet URI reproduces the torrent's announce URLs in its
.B tr=
parameters, and a private tracker's announce URL commonly embeds a per-user
passkey, so treat
.B magnet_link
output as a credential: do not paste it verbatim into a public issue report or a
pastebin. Nothing is redacted \(em that would break the scripting contract \(em
so the redaction is yours to do. See
.B "snatcher torrent trackers"
for the same caution on the announce URLs themselves.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent files \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List the files inside a single torrent, identified by its numeric id or
40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent files
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no files prints
.B "No files."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the files are emitted as an array in torrent order, so an element's array
position is its
.B index
column.
.IP
File indices are
.BR 0 -based
and torrent-relative: the
.B index
column is the file's position within this torrent, counting from zero, and is
the same index space the per-file selection and priority settings use. Besides
the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. When omitted every column
is shown. Available columns:
.BR index ", " name ", " size ", " done ", " percent ", " priority ", " wanted .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent files update \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIindex\fR...
Change the download priority and the wanted/unwanted selection of individual
files inside a single torrent. Exactly one torrent is selected, by numeric id or
40-character SHA1 info-hash; file indices are torrent-relative, so an index set
cannot meaningfully span several torrents and none is accepted.
.IP
Indices are
.BR 0 -based
and are the same numbers the
.B index
column of
.B "snatcher torrent files"
prints. One or more
.I index
arguments follow the selector, and each may hold several comma-separated
tokens. A token is a single index
.RB ( 3 ),
an inclusive range
.RB ( 2\-7 ),
or the literal
.BR all .
Tokens may be mixed freely across arguments, e.g.
.BR "snatcher torrent files update 5 0,2\-4 9" ;
.B all
selects every file in the torrent and must be given on its own. A reversed
range, a negative or non-integer index, and an empty token are each errors
naming the offending argument. Every index is validated against the torrent's
file count before anything is written, so an out-of-range index leaves the
torrent untouched. A torrent with no files \(em a magnet whose metadata has not
arrived yet, for instance \(em rejects every index form,
.B all
included, rather than reporting a change that could not have happened.
.IP
At least one of
.BR \-\-priority ", " \-\-wanted ", or " \-\-unwanted
is required; an invocation with none of them is an error, not a no-op. After the
change the affected files are re-read from the server and printed in the same
columns, and with the same
.B \-\-fields
handling, as
.BR "snatcher torrent files" .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-priority " " \fIlevel\fR
Download priority for the selected files. One of
.BR low ", " normal ", " high .
.TP
.B \-\-wanted
Download the selected files. This is a switch, not a boolean setting:
.B \-\-wanted=false
is not a way to mark a file unwanted. It reads as "not given", so an invocation
carrying only that fails with the "nothing to change" error and writes nothing.
Use
.B \-\-unwanted
to deselect a file.
.TP
.B \-\-unwanted
Do not download the selected files. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-wanted ,
and a switch in the same way:
.B \-\-unwanted=false
is not a way to mark a file wanted; use
.BR \-\-wanted .
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order \(em the same columns as
.BR "snatcher torrent files" :
.BR index ", " name ", " size ", " done ", " percent ", " priority ", " wanted .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent peers \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List the peers a single torrent is currently connected to, identified by its
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one selector is required, so a
bare
.B snatcher torrent peers
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no connected peers prints
.B "No peers."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the peers are emitted as an array in the server's order.
.IP
The
.B client
and
.B status
columns are chosen by the remote peer, not by the server, so they are
neutralized of terminal control sequences in the plaintext output;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit them verbatim. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. When omitted every column
is shown. Available columns:
.BR address ", " port ", " client ", " progress ", " down ", " up ,
.BR encrypted ", " status .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent trackers \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR
List a single torrent's trackers and their per-tracker announce stats,
identified by its numeric id or 40-character SHA1 info-hash. Exactly one
selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent trackers
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no trackers prints
.B "No trackers."
on a terminal and nothing when piped. With
.B \-\-json
the trackers are emitted as an array in the server's order.
.IP
The
.B state
column names the announce state
.RB ( inactive ", " waiting ", " queued ", " active );
.B \-\-json
emits the underlying integer. Transmission reports
.B \-1
for a seeder, leecher, or download count it has not scraped yet; those render as
.B \-
in the plaintext columns and stay
.B \-1
in
.BR \-\-json .
Announce URLs, hosts, site names, and last announce results come from the
torrent's metadata or from the tracker itself, not from the operator, so they
are neutralized of terminal control sequences in the plaintext output;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit them verbatim.
.IP
A private tracker's announce URL commonly embeds a per-user passkey, and
.B announce
is one of the default columns, so treat this output as a credential: do not
paste it verbatim into a public issue report or a pastebin. Nothing is redacted
\(em that would break the scripting contract \(em so the redaction is yours to
do. The same applies to
.BR "snatcher torrent show \-\-fields magnet_link" ,
whose
.B tr=
parameters reproduce these URLs. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR announce ", " tier ", " state ", " next_announce ", " seeders ,
.BR leechers ", " downloads .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR host ", " sitename ", " last_result .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
.RB ( ls )
List torrents. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a header; when
piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-filter " " \fIstatus\fR
Filter by state (default
.BR all ).
One of
.BR all ", " active ", " inactive ", " downloading ", " seeding ,
.BR stopped ", " verifying ", " queued\-to\-verify ", " queued\-to\-download ,
.BR queued\-to\-seed ", " error .
.B active
and
.B inactive
go by current transfer rate rather than status:
.B active
is any torrent with a non-zero download or upload rate, and
.B inactive
is one with neither that is not stopped or errored.
.TP
.BR \-\-tracker " " \fIhost\fR
Show only torrents whose main tracker is
.I host
(case-insensitive, exact match, no port), as named in the TUI sidebar's
Trackers section. A torrent's main tracker is the first one in its lowest
tier; a host it lists only as a fallback does not match. Each torrent's
.B trackers
field in
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
output carries all of its tracker hosts, never the announce URLs, which on a private
tracker embed a passkey.
.TP
.BR \-\-search " " \fItext\fR
Show only torrents whose name contains
.I text
(case-insensitive).
.TP
.BR \-\-sort " " \fIfield\fR
Sort by field. One of
.BR queue ", " name ", " added ", " size ", " progress ", " ratio ", " speed .
Overrides
.BR client.list_sort ,
which defaults to
.BR queue .
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the sort order. Overrides
.BR client.list_reverse ,
which defaults to
.BR false .
Pass
.B \-\-reverse=false
to override a configured
.B true
for one invocation.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show. Available columns:
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", " status ", " done ", " size ", " down ,
.BR up ", " ratio ", " eta ", " peers ", " added ", " download_dir ,
.BR error ", " labels .
Overrides
.BR client.list_columns ,
which defaults to
.BR id,name,status,done,size,down,up,ratio,eta .
Column selection applies to the plaintext output only:
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
always emit whole torrent objects, so a script sees every field regardless of
.B \-\-fields
or
.BR client.list_columns .
.BR \-\-sort ", " \-\-reverse ", " client.list_sort ", and " client.list_reverse
do apply to the structured paths \(em only the choice of columns is
plaintext-only.
.TP
.BR \-\-top " " \fIn\fR
Show only the first
.I n
rows of the final rendered order \(em that is, after
.BR \-\-filter ", " \-\-tracker ", " \-\-search ", " \-\-sort ", and " \-\-reverse
have been applied, so
.B "\-\-sort size \-\-reverse \-\-top 10"
is the ten largest torrents. Truncation applies to
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
output as well as plaintext, so a scripted consumer receives the same shortened
set a human sees. Omitting the flag is unlimited;
.I n
must be at least 1. There is no
.B client.*
key for it \(em
.B \-\-top
is a per-invocation question, not a stored preference.
.RE
.IP
Each of
.BR \-\-sort ", " \-\-reverse ", and " \-\-fields
takes effect only for the invocation it is passed to; use
.B snatcher config set
to change the corresponding
.B client.*
key for good.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent start \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Start or resume the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Start every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-now
Bypass the queue and start immediately.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent stop \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( pause )
Stop (pause) the selected torrents. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Stop every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent verify \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Verify local torrent data by queueing a re-hash of the downloaded files against
the torrent metadata. The bulk forms
.RB ( \-\-all " and " \-\-all\-with\-errors )
confirm first, naming the number of torrents: on a terminal they prompt
.RB [ y/N ],
and when standard input is not a terminal they refuse unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. An explicit id or hash selection is never prompted. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Verify every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-with\-errors
Verify every torrent the server currently reports an error for (the same set as
.BR "snatcher torrent list \-\-filter error" ).
Cannot be combined with
.B \-\-all
or with positional selectors. When no torrent has an error nothing is verified,
which is not an error: the plaintext path prints
.BR "No torrents with errors." ,
and
.BR \-\-json " / " \-\-jq
see a result with a count of 0 and an empty torrent list.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent reannounce \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reannounce the selected torrents to their trackers immediately, rather than
waiting for the next scheduled announce interval. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Reannounce every torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent move \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIlocation\fR
.RB ( mv )
Move a single torrent's download location. Select the torrent by numeric id or
40-character SHA1 hash. By default the existing files are relocated to
.IR location .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
Repoint Transmission at a path where the files already exist (after a manual
move) without relocating them.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent rename \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIid | hash\fR \fIpath\fR \fInew-name\fR
Rename a file or directory inside a single torrent. Select the torrent by
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 hash.
.I path
is the torrent-relative path of a file
.I or
directory, exactly as shown by
.BR "snatcher torrent files" ;
.I new-name
replaces its last component.
.IP
Renaming the torrent's root path
.RB ( path
equal to the torrent's own name) renames the torrent itself. That case prompts
.RB [ y/N ]
on a terminal, and when standard input is not a terminal it is refused unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. After a root rename, later paths are relative to the new root.
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue \fR[\fIid | hash\fR]...
Reorder the selected torrents within the download/seed queue. Select torrents by
numeric id or 40-character SHA1 hash; at least one selector is required (there
is no
.B \-\-all
flag, and a bare invocation is an error, not a no-op). Each subcommand accepts
the common options and confirms by naming the affected torrents. With no
subcommand it prints help.
.RS
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue top \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the top (front) of the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue up \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents up one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue down \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents down one position in the queue.
.TP
.B snatcher torrent queue bottom \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Move the selected torrents to the bottom (back) of the queue.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent remove \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( rm )
Remove the selected torrents from the session. Removal is destructive and
requires confirmation: on a terminal it prompts
.RB [ y/N ];
when standard input is not a terminal it refuses unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Remove every torrent.
.TP
.B \-\-delete\-data
Also delete the downloaded local files.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent update \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Change mutable per-torrent fields on the selected torrents. Select torrents by
numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all .
At least one selector and at least one field flag are required; a bare
invocation is an error, not a no-op. Only the fields whose flags are explicitly
given are written, so unspecified fields are left unchanged. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Update the fields on every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-priority " " \fIlow | normal | high\fR
Bandwidth priority for the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-queue\-position " " \fIn\fR
Position in the download/seed queue (0-based).
.TP
.BR \-\-peer\-limit " " \fIn\fR
Maximum number of peers for the torrent (1 or greater).
.TP
.BR \-\-honors\-session\-limits [= true | false ]
Whether the torrent honors the global session speed limits.
.TP
.BR \-\-sequential\-download [= true | false ]
Download pieces in sequential order (Transmission 4.1+).
.TP
.BR \-\-download\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent download speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-upload\-limit " " \fIkBps | off\fR
Per-torrent upload speed limit in kB/s, enabling the limit; the literal
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-ratio " " \fIratio | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent seed ratio limit. A number sets the limit;
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without a ratio limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-seed\-idle " " \fIminutes | duration | global | unlimited\fR
Per-torrent idle-seeding limit, as whole minutes (a bare integer or a Go-style
duration such as
.IR 30m ", " 1h ", or " 1h30m );
.B global
follows the session default;
.B unlimited
seeds without an idle limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to apply, replacing the torrent's entire label set. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-labels .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-labels
Remove all labels from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-tracker " " \fIurl\fR
Announce URL, replacing the torrent's entire tracker list. May be repeated.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-clear\-trackers .
.TP
.B \-\-clear\-trackers
Remove all trackers from the torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-group " " \fIname\fR
Bandwidth group name for the torrent.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent label
Read and change the labels on one or more torrents. Labels are free-form
strings Transmission stores per torrent; matching is exact and case-sensitive,
so
.B Foo
and
.B foo
are two distinct labels. With no subcommand it prints help.
.IP
This group is a wrapper over the same
.B labels
field
.B "snatcher torrent update"
writes. Unlike
.BR \-\-label / \-\-clear\-labels ,
which replace a torrent's whole label set,
.B add
and
.B remove
merge into the labels each torrent already has \(em the read\-modify\-write
happens here rather than in your shell, across a whole selection \(em while
.B list
reads the labels back as a command in its own right rather than as a column of
.BR "snatcher torrent list" .
.RS
.TP
.B snatcher torrent label list \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( ls )
List the labels on the selected torrents. Select torrents by numeric id,
40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent label list
is an error, not a no-op. On a terminal the output is an aligned table with a
header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A torrent with no labels renders an empty cell, so the
piped form stays parseable. With
.B \-\-json
each torrent is emitted as an object carrying
.BR id ", " hash ", " name ", and " labels ,
where
.B labels
is always an array \(em
.B []
for a torrent with no labels, never
.BR null .
Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
List the labels on every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR id ", " name ", " labels .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR hash .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent label add \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Add the given labels to the selected torrents,
.B keeping the labels each torrent already has.
Select torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent label add
with no selector is an error, not a no-op. At least one
.B \-\-label
is required too \(em there is no coherent "add nothing", so clearing a
selection is
.BR "snatcher torrent label set" 's
job. No confirmation is asked for, even under
.BR \-\-all .
.IP
.B \-\-label
may be repeated, and each occurrence is exactly one label: the value is never
split on commas. A label is trimmed of surrounding whitespace, and one that is
empty afterwards, or that contains a comma, is rejected before anything is
sent. Matching is exact and case-sensitive, so adding
.B Foo
to a torrent holding
.B foo
leaves it with both. The set that gets written is de-duplicated and sorted
byte-wise, so the order the labels were given in is not preserved.
.IP
Adding a label a torrent already has
.B changes nothing
for that torrent: it is left out of the write entirely, and when no torrent
would change nothing is sent at all. Torrents that do change are grouped by
their resulting labels and written one group at a time, so a selection landing
on a single resulting set costs one request however many torrents it names.
.IP
That grouping is also why the write is
.BR "not atomic" .
Resolving the selector is all-or-nothing: a token that matched no torrent is
caught before anything is sent, so a typo never half-applies a batch. The
writes themselves are not \(em a selection that lands on more than one
resulting label set is sent as one request per set, and a failure partway
through leaves the groups already sent applied while the rest are untouched.
Recovering is just re-running the same command: adding a label a torrent
already has changes nothing, so the groups that did land are skipped the second
time round.
.IP
The labels are read, merged locally, and written back. Transmission's RPC has
no compare-and-set, so another client changing a torrent's labels between that
read and the write loses, and this command does not guard against it. After the
write the labels are re-read from the server and printed in the same columns as
.BR "snatcher torrent label list" .
With
.B \-\-json
the output is the mutation contract \(em
.BR action ", " count ", and " torrents ,
where each torrent additionally carries its post-write
.BR labels ,
always an array. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Add the labels to every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to add, keeping the torrent's existing labels. May be repeated. Required.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR id ", " name ", " labels .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR hash .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent label remove \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
.RB ( rm )
Remove the given labels from the selected torrents,
.B leaving the other labels each torrent has alone.
Select torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent label remove
with no selector is an error, not a no-op. At least one
.B \-\-label
is required too \(em
.B remove
never falls back to stripping every label, so clearing a selection is
.BR "snatcher torrent label set" 's
job (run it with no
.BR \-\-label ).
No confirmation is asked for, even under
.BR \-\-all .
.IP
.B \-\-label
may be repeated, and each occurrence is exactly one label: the value is never
split on commas. A label is trimmed of surrounding whitespace, and one that is
empty afterwards, or that contains a comma, is rejected before anything is
sent. Matching is exact and case-sensitive, so removing
.B foo
from a torrent holding
.B Foo
leaves it alone. The set that gets written is de-duplicated and sorted
byte-wise, so the order of a torrent's remaining labels is not preserved.
.IP
Removing a label a torrent does not have
.B changes nothing
for that torrent: it is left out of the write entirely and is not an error, and
when no torrent would change nothing is sent at all. Torrents that do change are
grouped by their resulting labels and written one group at a time, so a
selection landing on a single resulting set costs one request however many
torrents it names. Removing a torrent's last label leaves it with no labels at
all.
.IP
That grouping is also why the write is
.BR "not atomic" .
Resolving the selector is all-or-nothing: a token that matched no torrent is
caught before anything is sent, so a typo never half-applies a batch. The
writes themselves are not \(em a selection that lands on more than one
resulting label set is sent as one request per set, and a failure partway
through leaves the groups already sent applied while the rest are untouched.
Recovering is just re-running the same command: removing a label a torrent no
longer has changes nothing, so the groups that did land are skipped the second
time round.
.IP
The labels are read, filtered locally, and written back. Transmission's RPC has
no compare-and-set, so another client changing a torrent's labels between that
read and the write loses, and this command does not guard against it. After the
write the labels are re-read from the server and printed in the same columns as
.BR "snatcher torrent label list" .
With
.B \-\-json
the output is the mutation contract \(em
.BR action ", " count ", and " torrents ,
where each torrent additionally carries its post-write
.BR labels ,
always an array and
.B []
for a torrent left with none. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Remove the labels from every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to remove, leaving the torrent's other labels. May be repeated. Required.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR id ", " name ", " labels .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR hash .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher torrent label set \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIid | hash\fR]...
Replace the labels on the selected torrents with the labels given. Select
torrents by numeric id, 40-character SHA1 hash, or
.BR \-\-all ;
at least one selector is required, so a bare
.B snatcher torrent label set
with no selector is an error, not a no-op. No confirmation is asked for, even
under
.BR \-\-all .
.IP
.B \-\-label
may be repeated, and each occurrence is exactly one label: the value is never
split on commas. A label is trimmed of surrounding whitespace, and one that is
empty afterwards, or that contains a comma, is rejected before anything is sent
\(em Transmission rejects both, and the plaintext renderer joins labels with a
comma. The set that gets written is de-duplicated (exact, case-sensitive match)
and sorted byte-wise, so the order the labels were given in is not preserved.
.IP
With no
.B \-\-label
at all,
.B snatcher torrent label set
.B removes every label
from the selected torrents. That is how a selection is cleared \(em but it also
means a mistyped
.B snatcher torrent label set 5
empties torrent 5's labels rather than erroring.
.IP
A torrent whose labels already match is left out of the write entirely; when no
torrent would change, nothing is sent at all. After the write the labels are
re-read from the server and printed in the same columns as
.BR "snatcher torrent label list" .
With
.B \-\-json
the output is the mutation contract \(em
.BR action ", " count ", and " torrents ,
where each torrent additionally carries its post-write
.BR labels ,
always an array and
.B []
when cleared. Besides the common options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Replace the labels on every torrent.
.TP
.BR \-\-label " " \fIname\fR
Label to write, replacing the torrent's labels. May be repeated. Omit entirely
to remove every label.
.TP
.BR \-\-fields " " \fIlist\fR
Comma-separated columns to show, in the given order. The default set is
.BR id ", " name ", " labels .
Also available, but not shown by default:
.BR hash .
.RE
.RE
.SS "config"
Read and change snatcher's own settings \(em how snatcher itself behaves, as opposed to
which server it talks to. They live in the
.B client
object of the config file (see
.BR FILES )
and are addressed on the command line by dotted keys such as
.BR client.theme .
The
.B config
group covers that section only; named servers are managed with
.B snatcher server
instead.
.TP
.B snatcher config list \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List every client setting, sorted by key, with its effective value and the
source that value came from:
.B flag
when a command-line option supplied it for this invocation,
.B env
when an environment variable did,
.B config
when the key is set in the config file, and
.B default
when none of them is. That is the precedence order, so the listing answers what
this invocation would actually use rather than only what the file holds.
Plaintext is an aligned KEY VALUE SOURCE table on a terminal and
tab-separated, header-less rows when piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set (the same contract as
.BR list ).
Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher config get \fIkey\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Print the effective value of one setting. The plaintext output is the bare value
with nothing else \(em no key name, padding, or decoration \(em so it can be
captured directly in a shell, e.g.\&
.IR "theme=$(snatcher config get client.theme)" ;
a list-valued key prints comma-separated.
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit the same
.IR key ", " value ", " source
object that
.B config list
emits per row, with the value in its natural JSON type. The value is resolved
through the same
.B flag
\(->
.B env
\(->
.B config
\(->
.B default
chain
.B config list
reports. An unknown key is an
error listing the valid keys. A value in the config file that is not valid for
its key is an error naming the key, the offending value, and what is accepted;
it is never silently replaced by the default. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher config set \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Store one setting in the config file. The value is parsed and checked before
anything is written, so an unknown key or a value the key does not accept is an
error naming the key, the offending value, and what is accepted, and the config
file is left exactly as it was. A list-valued key such as
.B client.list_columns
takes its elements comma-separated, in order; an empty list, an unknown element,
and a repeated element are all rejected. Everything else in the file \(em the
.B servers
map, the
.BR default_server ,
and any key snatcher does not recognize \(em is carried through untouched, and a
config file that does not exist yet is created (mode 0600, its parent 0700)
rather than refused. A value that begins with a dash, such as a negative
duration, must follow a
.B \-\-
terminator. On success the key and its new value are reported;
.B \-\-json
and
.B \-\-jq
emit the same
.IR key ", " value ", " source
object the read commands emit. Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher config unset \fIkey\fR \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
Remove one setting from the config file, so the key reverts to its default.
Unsetting a key that is not set succeeds and changes nothing, so it is safe to
re-run. The reported value is the default the key falls back to, with source
.BR default .
Accepts
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq .
.PP
.B set
and
.B unset
resolve only the key they are changing, so a value hand-edited into some other
key that is not valid for it never blocks the repair: a config file is always
fixable from the command line.
.PP
The settings, with the defaults that apply when they are not set:
.TP
.B client.theme
Color theme, one of
.BR default ", " dracula ", " nord ", or " solarized .
Default:
.BR default .
.TP
.B client.nerd_font
Whether to draw Nerd Font glyphs rather than ASCII icons
.RB ( true / false ).
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.B client.poll_interval
How often the interface refreshes its data, in Go duration syntax (e.g.\&
.IR 5s ", " 1m30s ),
between 1s and 1h. Default:
.BR 5s .
.TP
.B client.list_columns
Comma-separated default columns for
.BR "torrent list" ,
in order, named from the same set as its
.B \-\-fields
option. Default:
.BR id,name,status,done,size,down,up,ratio,eta .
.TP
.B client.list_sort
Default sort field for
.BR "torrent list" ,
named from the same set as its
.B \-\-sort
option. Default:
.BR queue .
.TP
.B client.list_reverse
Whether
.B "torrent list"
reverses its sort order by default
.RB ( true / false ).
Default:
.BR false .
.PP
.BR client.theme " and " client.nerd_font
can also be overridden for a single invocation, without editing the file, with
.BR \-\-theme " and " \-\-no\-nerd\-font
or with
.BR SNATCHER_THEME " and " SNATCHER_NO_NERD_FONT .
An override applies to what
.BR "config list" " and " "config get"
report and to nothing else:
.BR set " and " unset
write the file and are unaffected by whatever is in force, so a
.B \-\-theme
on the command line can never end up persisted.
.PP
.BR client.theme ", " client.nerd_font ", and " client.poll_interval
are the TUI's own settings, and no other command renders them:
.B client.theme
picks its palette,
.B client.nerd_font
whether it draws Nerd Font glyphs or their ASCII fallbacks, and
.B client.poll_interval
how often it refreshes server data.
.SS "search"
.B snatcher search
.RI [ options "] [" query ]
.br
Search the indexers a configured Prowlarr instance knows about. This is the one
command group that talks to Prowlarr rather than to Transmission, so its
.B \-\-server
names a configured
.B prowlarr
alias (see
.BR "snatcher server add \-\-type" );
with it omitted the configured
.B default_prowlarr_server
is used, and its API key is read from that server's secret store or from
.BR SNATCHER_API_KEY .
.PP
The query is a positional argument, or
.B \-\-query
for the same thing. Supplying both is an error, and supplying neither is an
error naming both forms.
.PP
.B search
is the only group that both runs an action of its own and carries subcommands,
so a subcommand name wins over a bare positional query:
.B "snatcher search indexers"
runs the subcommand rather than searching for the word
.IR indexers ,
and the same holds for
.BR grab ", " download ", and " categories .
That is deliberate \(em it keeps the common case,
.BR "snatcher search ubuntu" ,
the shortest thing to type \(em and
.B \-\-query
is the complete escape:
.B "snatcher search \-\-query indexers"
searches for the literal word. The subcommands are listed under
.B "snatcher search \-\-help"
with the same note.
.PP
Results are rendered in Prowlarr's own order across the indexers it queried;
snatcher does not sort, filter, or de-duplicate them. On a terminal the output
is an aligned table with the columns
.RB ( # ", " AGE ", " TITLE ", " INDEXER ", " SIZE ", " GRABS ", " SEEDERS ", " LEECHERS );
when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header \(em a stable
scripting contract. A search that matches nothing prints
.B "No results."
on a terminal, nothing when piped, and
.B []
with
.BR \-\-json ,
exiting 0 in all three cases.
.PP
.B AGE
is whole days since the release was published, and
.BR GRABS ", " SEEDERS ", and " LEECHERS
render as a dash when the indexer does not publish the count \(em a usenet
release has no seeders \(em which is distinct from a published zero. The
leading
.B #
column numbers the rendered order from 1 and is present as the
.B index
field in
.BR \-\-json / \-\-jq
output, so a human reading the table and a script reading JSON name a release
the same way.
.PP
Prowlarr hands back a
.B download_url
that proxies through Prowlarr itself and carries the API key as a query
parameter, because a torrent client fetching that url has nowhere to put a
header; an indexer may build
.BR info_url " or " comment_url
the same way. The value of any
.B apikey
parameter is therefore replaced with
.B REDACTED
in the
.BR \-\-json / \-\-jq
output of
.B search
and
.BR "search grab" ,
so a filtered result can be logged or pasted without leaking the key. The
rewrite is made on the way out only:
.B "snatcher search download"
fetches the url Prowlarr gave it, and
.B "snatcher search grab"
hands Prowlarr back the exact release resource it received. Besides the common
options:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-query " " \fIterm\fR
The search query, the flag form of the positional argument.
.TP
.BR \-\-indexer " " \fIid\fR
Restrict the search to the indexer with this numeric id, as Prowlarr numbers
its configured indexers. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-category " " \fIid\fR
Restrict the search to this torznab category id. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-limit " " \fIn\fR
Maximum results to request per page. Always sent, so paging steps a
deterministic offset. Default:
.BR 100 .
.TP
.BR \-\-offset " " \fIn\fR
Results to skip before the first page. Default:
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR \-\-pages " " \fIn\fR
Request
.I n
sequential pages, each stepping the offset by
.BR \-\-limit ,
concatenating the results in order. A page returning fewer results than it
asked for ends the walk, so the remaining pages cost no round trips. Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BR \-\-search\-type " " \fItype\fR
The torznab search type: one of
.BR search ", " tvsearch ", " movie ", " music ", or " book .
An indexer may implement only some of the typed searches. Default:
.BR search .
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher search grab \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIquery\fR] [\fIn\fR]
Hand one release from a search back to Prowlarr to grab.
.IP
A grab is handed to Prowlarr's
.I own
configured download client, which is not necessarily the Transmission server
snatcher is pointed at. It means \(lqtell Prowlarr to grab it\(rq, not \(lqadd
it to this server\(rq. To add a release to the server snatcher is pointed at,
feed its download or magnet url to
.BR "snatcher torrent add" .
.IP
The release is named in one of two mutually exclusive ways.
.B "snatcher search grab \(dqubuntu\(dq 3"
re-runs the search and resolves the 1-based result number \(em the
.B #
column \(em from the fresh results; snatcher caches no result state between
invocations. That number therefore names the release that was on screen only
when the same
.BR \-\-indexer ", " \-\-category ", " \-\-limit ", " \-\-offset ", " \-\-pages ", and " \-\-search\-type
are passed, and only while the results have not shifted underneath it. A number
below 1, or past the end of the results, is an error naming the number and how
many results came back.
.IP
.B "snatcher search grab \-\-guid \fIguid\fB \-\-indexer \fIid\fR"
is the exact form: it names the release outright and re-runs nothing, so
neither caveat applies. Every
.B guid
appears in
.B "snatcher search \-\-json"
output for exactly this reason, which makes it the form a script should use.
.IP
The resolved release's title and indexer are printed to standard error before
the grab is issued, so what is being acted on is on screen in every output mode
and a shifted result set is visible rather than silent. A single named release
is never prompted for. On success the plaintext path prints a confirmation line
naming the release Prowlarr accepted, while
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
emit the grabbed release object instead. Titles and indexer names are
server-derived and are neutralized of terminal control sequences on the
plaintext paths only. Besides the common options and the search filters listed
above:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-guid " " \fIguid\fR
Name the release exactly by the guid its indexer assigned it, instead of by
position in a re-run. Requires exactly one
.BR \-\-indexer ,
naming the indexer that issued the guid, since a guid is unique only within one
indexer; zero or more than one is an error. Takes no positional arguments.
.RE
.TP
.B snatcher search download \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIquery\fR] [\fIn\fR]
Write one release's .torrent file to disk.
.IP
This is the local counterpart of
.BR "snatcher search grab" :
nothing is handed to a download client, the bytes simply land on this machine,
ready for
.B "snatcher torrent add"
or anywhere else. The release is named by position in a re-run of the search,
exactly as
.B grab
names it, and the resolved release is echoed to standard error before it is
fetched \(em with
.B "\-\-output \-"
that echo is the only thing that says what the bytes are.
.IP
.B \-\-guid
is accepted for symmetry with
.BR grab ,
but it cannot download: a guid names a release without describing one, and
Prowlarr resolves it only when grabbing, so there is no download url on it to
fetch. The error says so and points at the positional form.
.IP
The bytes come from the release's own download url, which may be served by
Prowlarr or straight by the indexer; the Prowlarr API key travels only when
that url is on the Prowlarr instance's own origin, and is dropped again at any
redirect that leaves it. A release publishing only a
.B magnet_url
has no file to write, and the error hands over the
.B \-\-jq
expression that extracts the link. Besides the common options and the search
filters listed above:
.RS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIpath\fR
Write to
.I path
instead of to a derived filename, or to standard output when
.I path
is
.BR \- .
Writing to standard output prints nothing else \(em not the confirmation line,
not
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-\-guid " " \fIguid\fR
Accepted as it is by
.BR "snatcher search grab" ,
and always an error here; see above.
.RE
.IP
With no
.BR \-\-output ,
the filename is derived from the release
.I title
and written to the current directory. It is never taken from the response's
.B Content\-Disposition
header, which is a server-controlled string naming a file on this machine. The
title is reduced to a single safe filename \(em path separators, leading dots,
and control bytes are removed, and the name is bounded in length \(em with a
.B .torrent
extension, so an indexer can neither write outside the current directory nor
put an escape sequence into a name every
.B ls
will echo back.
.IP
An existing file is never replaced: that is an error naming the path and
suggesting
.BR \-\-output ,
and there is deliberately no
.B \-\-force
to override it. The file is created at mode 0600 \(em a private tracker's
.B .torrent
embeds a passkey \(em and written atomically, through a temporary file in the
destination directory that is renamed into place, so an interrupted download
leaves no partial
.B .torrent
behind. On success the plaintext path prints the written path and size, while
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
emit
.BR path ", " bytes ", " title ", and " guid .
.TP
.B snatcher search indexers \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List the indexers the Prowlarr instance is configured with, in the server's own
order, as
.BR ID ", " NAME ", " PROTOCOL ", " PRIVACY ", " ENABLED ", and " PRIORITY .
.B ID
is the number
.B \-\-indexer
takes, which is what this listing is for: Prowlarr's web UI names indexers, and
nothing else in snatcher maps a name to an id.
.B ENABLED
and
.B PRIORITY
explain a search's results \(em a disabled indexer contributes nothing to a
search that does not name it, and priority is how Prowlarr breaks ties. On a
terminal the output is an aligned table with a header; when piped (or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set) it is tab-separated, full-value rows with no header. An instance with no
indexers prints
.B "No indexers configured."
on a terminal, nothing when piped, and
.B []
with
.BR \-\-json .
Names, protocols, and privacy levels come from the server, so they are
neutralized of terminal control sequences in the plaintext output;
.BR \-\-json " and " \-\-jq
emit them verbatim. A private indexer's settings hold its passkey or API key;
those fields are never decoded, so no output mode can print them. Accepts
.BR \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.TP
.B snatcher search categories \fR[\fIoptions\fR]
List the torznab categories the Prowlarr instance knows about as
.BR ID " and " NAME ,
where
.B ID
is the number
.B \-\-category
takes. Prowlarr returns the categories as a tree; the listing flattens it
depth-first in the server's own order, so a parent is always the row above its
first child. On a terminal a sub-category is indented under its parent; when
piped the name is the server's own, undecorated \(em the piped rows are a
full-value scripting contract, and a torznab category name already spells its
parent
.RB ( Movies/HD " under " Movies ).
With
.BR \-\-json " or " \-\-jq
each row carries
.BR id ", " name ", " description ", and " parent_id ,
the last being
.B null
for a top-level category; the nested tree itself is not emitted, since every
child is already its own row. The full default tree is listed, not only the
categories the configured indexers happen to support. An instance with no
categories prints
.B "No categories."
on a terminal, nothing when piped, and
.B []
with
.BR \-\-json .
Category names are server-derived and are neutralized of terminal control
sequences in the plaintext output only. Accepts
.BR \-\-server ", " \-\-json ", and " \-\-jq .
.SS "api"
.B snatcher api
.RI [ options "] " method
.br
.B snatcher api \-\-prowlarr
.RI [ options "] " path
.br
Power-user escape hatch, modeled on
.BR "gh api" ,
that issues an arbitrary Transmission RPC
.I method
(canonical kebab-case, e.g.\&
.IR session\-get ", " torrent\-get ", " queue\-move\-top )
and prints a normalized response envelope that is identical whether the server
speaks the bespoke dialect (Transmission 4.0 and earlier) or JSON-RPC 2.0
(4.1 and later). snatcher translates the method name only; it never rewrites
argument keys or values, which are not portable across dialect versions. An
RPC-level failure still prints the envelope, then exits non-zero.
.PP
With
.B \-\-prowlarr
the positional is a Prowlarr REST
.I path
instead, issued against the configured Prowlarr server. A
.I path
beginning with
.B /
is used verbatim against that server's base URL; a bare one is expanded to
.IB /api/v1/ path \fR,
where every documented endpoint lives. This is how the parts of Prowlarr's API
.B "snatcher search"
does not model \(em history, statistics, notifications, indexer management \(em
stay reachable. Options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-prowlarr
Treat the positional as a Prowlarr REST path and call the configured Prowlarr
server.
.B \-\-server
must then name a
.B prowlarr
alias.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-method " " \fIverb\fR
HTTP method for
.BR \-\-prowlarr :
one of
.BR GET " (default), " HEAD ", " POST ", " PUT ", " PATCH ", " DELETE ", or " OPTIONS ,
matched case-insensitively. It is an error without
.BR \-\-prowlarr ,
since a Transmission RPC call is always a POST of the method name.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a type-inferred argument field:
.BR true ", " false ", and " null
become JSON literals, numbers become JSON numbers,
.IR @file " or " @\-
read a file or standard input as a string, and anything else is a string. The
.I key:=value
form parses the value as raw JSON. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-raw\-field " " \fIkey=value\fR
Add a string argument field; the value is always a literal string. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-input " " \fIfile\fR
Supply the entire arguments object as raw JSON from
.IR file ,
or
.B \-
for standard input. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-field " and " \-\-raw\-field .
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Print the server's exact response bytes, bypassing normalization. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-jq .
.RE
.PP
The
.B api
command also accepts
.BR \-\-jq ;
it has no
.B \-\-json
flag, since the normalized envelope is already JSON.
.PP
.B "The two modes print different shapes."
A Transmission call prints the normalized
.BR result / arguments / tag
envelope, so a
.B \-\-jq
expression addresses the payload as \fI.arguments\fR.
A Prowlarr call has no envelope to normalize \(em its REST API answers with the
resource itself \(em so the response body is printed as indented JSON exactly as
the server sent it, and a
.B \-\-jq
expression addresses that body directly
.RI ( .[].name ", not " .arguments.[].name ).
.B \-\-raw
prints the exact response bytes in both modes \(em save for the API-key
substitution described below.
A body that is not JSON at all (an HTML page from a reverse proxy, or the empty
body of a successful
.BR DELETE )
has nothing to indent or filter, so the body itself is the output; on the
default path it is sanitized like any other server-derived text, and only
.B \-\-raw
writes its escape sequences through untouched.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-prowlarr
the field flags follow the verb.
.BR \-f ", " \-F ", and " \-\-input
become
.B "query parameters"
for
.BR GET " and " DELETE
\(em a repeated key stays repeated, which is how Prowlarr binds an array
parameter such as
.IR indexerIds ,
and an
.B \-\-input
document must be a JSON object, whose top-level keys become the parameters.
For every other verb they become a
.B "JSON request body"
instead, with
.B \-\-input
supplying that body verbatim. A non-2xx response prints the server's own body,
then exits non-zero, mirroring how the Transmission mode prints its envelope
before failing. The Prowlarr API key travels as a header and never reaches the
output, including under
.BR \-\-raw :
an instance hands its own key back in ordinary answers \(em a release's
.I downloadUrl
is a proxy link carrying
.BR apikey= ,
and
.B config/host
reports the key in a field \(em so the configured key is replaced with
.B REDACTED
wherever it appears in the response body. A body that does not carry it is
printed byte for byte.
.SS "version, completion, help"
.B snatcher version
prints version information.
.B snatcher completion
generates a shell completion script for bash, zsh, fish, or PowerShell.
.B snatcher help
.RI [ command ]
shows help for any command.
.SH OUTPUT
When a command can emit structured output, the render mode is chosen with this
precedence:
.BR \-\-jq " (highest), then " \-\-json ", then plaintext (default)."
The
.B json
field tags on the underlying types are a stable scripting surface; treat them
as an API. Human and plaintext output passes server-derived strings through a
terminal sanitizer; JSON and jq output does not, as it is a machine contract.
.PP
Tab-separated plaintext is part of that contract too, and it grew once, on
purpose:
.B "snatcher server list"
gained a
.B TYPE
column in position 2
.RB ( "NAME TYPE URL USERNAME SECRET DEFAULT" )
when Prowlarr servers became registrable, so a script splitting those rows on
tabs reads one more field than it used to. Its
.B \-\-json
counterpart gained the same value as an additive
.B type
key, which is the form to prefer in a script.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred: the server was unreachable, authentication failed, the
selectors matched no torrents, a confirmation was refused, or at least one
.B add
source failed.
.SH SECRETS
A Transmission server's password and a Prowlarr instance's API key are the only
secrets snatcher holds, and neither ever goes in the config file. Each server
names the store its secret lives in, chosen with
.B \-\-store
at
.B "snatcher server add"
time, saved as
.B secret_store
in the config file, and overridable for one invocation with
.BR SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE .
Everything below is written in terms of a password; a Prowlarr API key is
stored, read, reported, warned about, and deleted through exactly the same
paths, with
.B SNATCHER_API_KEY
standing in for
.BR SNATCHER_PASSWORD .
.TP
.B keyring
The operating system keyring (macOS Keychain, or the Secret Service on Linux),
under the service name
.B snatcher
keyed by the server URL. This is the default and the only store snatcher will
use unless told otherwise.
.TP
.B file
A
.B plaintext
JSON file at
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json ,
keyed by server URL, created with mode 0600 under a 0700 directory. It exists
for machines with no OS keyring and is
.B less safe
than the keyring: anything that can read the file (a backup, another process
running as you, a misconfigured sync) reads the password. Nothing writes to it
unless it is asked for by name.
.TP
.B none
No store at all: nothing is read and nothing is written. Supply the password
per invocation with
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
instead, or use a server with authentication disabled.
.PP
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
sits above every store on read paths, so it works in all three modes. It is
never written to any store.
.SS "When the secret store is unavailable"
Snatcher distinguishes
.I no entry for this server
from
.IR "the store could not be reached at all" .
The second is reported as the secret store being
.BR unavailable ,
and is expected on a headless Linux box: the Secret Service is reached over the
session D\-Bus, so with no D\-Bus session running there is nothing to answer,
and every keyring read and write fails. It is not a sign of a corrupt keyring
or a wrong password.
.PP
Read paths degrade rather than abort.
.BR "server list" ", " "server show" ", " "server remove" ,
and every command that connects to a server keep working; each prints a
one-line warning
on standard error and, where a password was needed, proceeds without one. If
the server does require authentication, the request then fails with the usual
authentication error.
.PP
.B "snatcher server add"
is the one exception, and only when a non-empty password was typed: it refuses
rather than register a server whose password went nowhere, and writes nothing
at all \(em no config entry and no secret. An empty password always succeeds,
since there is nothing to store. The three ways forward are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Install or start an OS secret service (on Linux, a Secret Service provider such
as gnome\-keyring, and a D\-Bus session for it to answer on).
.IP \(bu 2
Re-run with
.B \-\-store=file
to keep the password in the plaintext file store described above.
.IP \(bu 2
Re-run with
.B \-\-store=none
and supply the password per invocation with
.BR SNATCHER_PASSWORD ,
which leaves nothing at rest on disk.
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SNATCHER_SERVER
Selects the configured server alias to connect to, equivalent to passing
.BR \-\-server .
The
.B \-\-server
flag takes precedence; when neither is set, the configured default server for
the type the command needs is used.
.IP
It applies to Transmission and Prowlarr commands alike. Unlike
.BR SNATCHER_PASSWORD " and " SNATCHER_API_KEY ,
which are each scoped to one server type, this variable names one alias for
every command, so an alias of the wrong type is an error rather than a silent
fallback. A Transmission command that resolves a Prowlarr alias \(em or a
.B search
command, or
.B "snatcher api"
with
.BR \-\-prowlarr ,
that resolves a Transmission one \(em fails naming the alias, its actual type,
the type the command needs, and the fact that the alias came from
.BR SNATCHER_SERVER ,
instead of falling back to
.BR default_server " or " default_prowlarr_server .
.IP
That is a deliberate choice, not an oversight: quietly ignoring an exported
variable would leave no way to tell which server a command actually reached. To
run a command of the other type, pass
.B \-\-server
to override the variable for that invocation, or unset it and let the per-type
default apply.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
Selects the secret store passwords are read from and written to, for every
server, for this invocation. One of
.BR keyring ", " file ", or " none ,
with the same meanings as
.B \-\-store
on
.BR "snatcher server add" .
Precedence is
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
over the server's
.B secret_store
value in the config file, over
.BR keyring .
An unrecognized value is an error, never a silent fallback. An explicit
.B \-\-store
on
.B "snatcher server add"
outranks it, so the store saved with a server is always the one its password
was written to. It is also ignored wherever the question is about a secret a
server already has rather than about the password to use for this invocation:
.B "snatcher server remove"
and the clean-up
.B "snatcher server add"
performs when an alias's URL or store changes both delete from the store the
server itself names, since that is where the password actually is, and
.B "snatcher server list"
and
.B "snatcher server show"
report secret status from that same store, so an override cannot make a stored
password look absent.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
Supplies the password for this invocation, ahead of every secret store. When it
is set to a non-empty value, no store is read at all, whatever
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
or the server's
.B secret_store
names, so
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE=none
together with
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
is a fully unattended configuration with no password written to disk. It is a
read-only override:
.B "snatcher server add"
never stores its value, and an empty or unset value defers to the resolved
store.
.IP
Beware that a process environment is not private. On Linux any other process
running as the same user can read it (for example
.IR /proc/ PID /environ ),
it is inherited by every child process snatcher spawns, and it is commonly
captured in shell history and CI logs. Prefer the OS keyring where one is
available.
.IP
It applies to Transmission servers only. A Prowlarr instance takes its
credential from
.B SNATCHER_API_KEY
instead, and neither variable is ever consulted for the other type.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_API_KEY
Supplies the Prowlarr API key for this invocation, ahead of every secret store.
It is the Prowlarr counterpart to
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
and behaves identically: a non-empty value means no store is read at all,
whatever
.B SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE
or the server's
.B secret_store
names;
.B "snatcher server add"
never stores its value; and an empty or unset value defers to the resolved
store. It applies only to servers registered with
.BR "\-\-type prowlarr" ,
and
.B SNATCHER_PASSWORD
is not consulted for those. The same warning about the privacy of a process
environment applies.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_THEME
Selects the color theme the TUI draws with for this invocation, equivalent to
passing
.BR \-\-theme .
One of
.BR default " (the terminal's own sixteen colors), " dracula ", " nord ", or " solarized ;
each of the latter three has a light and a dark arrangement, chosen from the
terminal's background color.
The
.B \-\-theme
flag takes precedence; when neither is set, the
.B client.theme
setting is used, and failing that the built-in default. An unset or empty value
expresses no opinion and falls through. An unknown theme is an error naming the
variable and the valid names, never a silent fallback.
.TP
.B SNATCHER_NO_NERD_FONT
Draws ASCII icons rather than Nerd Font glyphs for this invocation, equivalent
to passing
.BR \-\-no\-nerd\-font .
Like the flag, and unlike the positive
.B client.nerd_font
setting, it is negative: a truthy value
.RB ( 1 ", " true )
turns glyphs
.IR off ,
and a falsy one turns them on. An unset or empty value expresses no opinion and
falls through to the config file. The
.B \-\-no\-nerd\-font
flag takes precedence. A value that is not a boolean is an error naming the
variable, never a silent fallback.
.IP
Both variables are the TUI's: they change how it draws itself and nothing else.
They are resolved and checked by every command that reads them, and
.B snatcher config list
reports a setting they supply with the source
.BR env .
A command that reads neither ignores them entirely, invalid value and all.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, forces
.B list
into the piped, tab-separated, header-less contract even on a terminal, and
draws the TUI without color: pane focus is shown by its border shape \(em
rounded when unfocused, thick when focused, double for the filter pane \(em and
the cursor row, the selected list row, and the status bar in reverse video, so
nothing that color carried is lost. The predicate is the conventional one: any
non-empty value, whatever it says.
.IP
A terminal that cannot show color, including
.BR TERM=dumb ,
is drawn the same way without the variable.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
When set, the default config path is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
When set, the default path of the
.B file
secret store is
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json .
It is consulted only in the
.B file
store mode; the keyring never touches it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snatcher/config.json
Non-secret configuration (a
.B servers
map of named server aliases, each with a
.BR url ,
a
.BR username ,
and an optional
.B type
of
.B transmission
or
.BR prowlarr ;
a
.B default_server
alias and a
.B default_prowlarr_server
alias, one default per type; and a
.B client
object holding snatcher's own settings \(em see
.BR "snatcher config" ),
created with mode 0600 under a 0700 directory. Both Prowlarr keys are written
only when they are needed: an absent
.B type
means
.BR transmission ,
and
.B default_prowlarr_server
appears only once a Prowlarr instance is registered, so a config file written
before Prowlarr support round-trips through a save unchanged. The file is
operator-owned and
hand-editable: keys snatcher does not recognize, at either level, survive a
rewrite untouched. Every write is atomic \(em the new content goes to a
temporary file created with mode 0600 in the same directory, which is then
renamed over the target, so an interrupted write cannot truncate the config.
One consequence is worth knowing: a rename
.I replaces
a symlink at that path rather than writing through it, so a
.I config.json
symlinked into a dotfiles repository is turned into a regular file by the first
.BR "snatcher config set" ", " "snatcher server add" ,
or any other command that saves. Falls back to
.I ~/.config/snatcher/config.json
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. Neither a server password nor a Prowlarr API key is ever written to
this file; it holds only the name of the store each server's secret lives in
.RB ( secret_store ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/snatcher/secrets.json
The
.B file
secret store: server passwords and Prowlarr API keys in
.BR plaintext ,
as a JSON object keyed by server URL. Written atomically with mode 0600 under a
0700 directory. Falls back to
.I ~/.local/state/snatcher/secrets.json
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset. This file is created only for a server explicitly registered with
.BR \-\-store=file " (or under " SNATCHER_SECRET_STORE=file );
the default
.B keyring
store never creates it, and never falls back to it when the keyring cannot be
reached.
.PP
In the default
.B keyring
store the secret is in no file at all: it lives in the operating system
keyring (macOS Keychain or the Linux Secret Service), under the service name
.B snatcher
keyed by the server URL. See
.B SECRETS
for how a store is chosen.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Configure and verify a server"
.EX
# point snatcher at your Transmission RPC endpoint (prompts for a password)
$ snatcher server add \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin

# read the password from stdin instead of a prompt (scripting)
$ printf '%s' "$TRANSMISSION_PASSWORD" | snatcher server add \(rs
    \-\-url http://localhost:9091/transmission/rpc \-\-username admin \-\-password\-stdin

# confirm the connection works
$ snatcher server ping
.EE
.SS "List and filter torrents"
.EX
# seeding torrents, highest ratio first
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-filter seeding \-\-sort ratio \-\-reverse

# pick the columns you care about
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-fields id,name,ratio,up

# search by name
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-search ubuntu

# the ten largest torrents
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-sort size \-\-reverse \-\-top 10

# names of torrents that are not finished, via jq
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-jq '.[] | select(.done < 100) | .name'
.EE
.SS "Start, stop, and remove torrents"
.EX
# start three torrents by id, jumping the queue
$ snatcher torrent start 1 2 3 \-\-now

# pause everything
$ snatcher torrent stop \-\-all

# remove a torrent and its data without prompting
$ snatcher torrent remove 5 \-\-delete\-data \-\-yes
.EE
.SS "Add torrents"
.EX
# add a file and a magnet link, label them, and start immediately
$ snatcher torrent add ubuntu.iso.torrent 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...' \(rs
    \-\-label linux \-\-label iso \-\-start

# add every source listed in a file (one per line)
$ snatcher torrent add < sources.txt
.EE
.SS "Manage labels"
.EX
# what labels does torrent 5 have?
$ snatcher torrent label list 5

# add a label to two torrents, keeping the labels they already have
$ snatcher torrent label add 5 12 \-\-label hd

# repeat \-\-label to add more than one at a time
$ snatcher torrent label add 5 \-\-label hd \-\-label movies

# take one label off every torrent that has it (torrents without it are
# untouched, and nothing is sent for them)
$ snatcher torrent label rm \-\-all \-\-label stale

# replace torrent 5's labels with exactly these two
$ snatcher torrent label set 5 \-\-label hd \-\-label movies

# a bare set with no \-\-label removes every label from torrent 5
$ snatcher torrent label set 5

# every torrent tagged hd, by name (script-friendly)
$ snatcher torrent label list \-\-all \(rs
    \-\-jq '.[] | select(.labels | index("hd")) | .name'
.EE
.SS "Rename a file or a torrent"
.EX
# find the torrent\-relative path, then rename that file
$ snatcher torrent files 3
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04/disk.iso' ubuntu.iso

# renaming the root path renames the torrent itself, so it prompts
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04' ubuntu\-24.04

# same, unattended (required when stdin is not a terminal)
$ snatcher torrent rename 3 'Ubuntu 24.04' ubuntu\-24.04 \-\-yes
.EE
.SS "Session statistics"
.EX
$ snatcher server stats
.EE
.SS "Inspect and change settings"
.EX
# show the connection, the server version, and every setting
$ snatcher server show

# show only the bandwidth settings
$ snatcher server show \-\-category bandwidth

# show the connection settings without contacting the server
$ snatcher server show \-\-local

# read one value (script-friendly)
$ port=$(snatcher server show \-\-jq \(rs
    '.settings[]|select(.key=="peer\-port").value')

# set the download speed limit to 500 KB/s and enable it
$ snatcher server update \-\-speed\-limit\-down 500 \-\-speed\-limit\-down\-enabled

# restore settings to their defaults
$ snatcher server reset peer\-port encryption
.EE
.SS "Speed limits and turtle mode"
.EX
# the whole picture: current rates, both limit pairs, and the alt switch
$ snatcher server bandwidth show

# set the download limit to 500 KB/s \(em this also turns the limit on
$ snatcher server bandwidth update download 500

# turn the upload limit off, keeping its stored value for next time
$ snatcher server bandwidth update upload off

# store the alt (turtle) download limit, without activating turtle mode
$ snatcher server bandwidth update download 50 \-\-alt

# activate turtle mode for both directions
$ snatcher server bandwidth alt toggle

# is turtle mode on? (script-friendly)
$ snatcher server bandwidth show \-\-jq '.alt_enabled'
.EE
.SS "Read and change snatcher's own settings"
.EX
# every client setting, its value, and where the value came from
$ snatcher config list

# one value, bare, for a shell
$ theme=$(snatcher config get client.theme)

# change a setting, then drop it back to its default
$ snatcher config set client.list_columns id,name,ratio
$ snatcher config unset client.list_columns

# the list defaults apply with no flags; a flag still wins for one invocation
$ snatcher config set client.list_sort ratio
$ snatcher torrent list
$ snatcher torrent list \-\-sort queue

# override an appearance setting for one invocation, without editing the file
$ snatcher config list \-\-theme nord
$ SNATCHER_NO_NERD_FONT=1 snatcher config get client.nerd_font
.EE
.SS "Search with Prowlarr"
.EX
# register a Prowlarr instance beside your Transmission server
# (prompts once, hidden, for the API key)
$ snatcher server add \-\-name prowlarr \-\-type prowlarr \(rs
    \-\-url http://localhost:9696

# read the API key from stdin instead of a prompt (scripting)
$ printf '%s' "$PROWLARR_API_KEY" | snatcher server add \-\-name prowlarr \(rs
    \-\-type prowlarr \-\-url http://localhost:9696 \-\-api\-key\-stdin

# confirm it answers, and see both servers with their types
$ snatcher server ping prowlarr
$ snatcher server list

# the indexer and category ids the filters below take
$ snatcher search indexers
$ snatcher search categories

# search two indexers and one category, three pages deep
$ snatcher search 'ubuntu 24.04' \-\-indexer 3 \-\-indexer 7 \(rs
    \-\-category 2000 \-\-pages 3

# every result's number, guid, and indexer id \(em the guid is what the exact
# grab form below takes
$ snatcher search 'ubuntu 24.04' \-\-jq '.[] | {index, guid, indexer_id}'

# hand one release to Prowlarr's own download client, named exactly, with no
# re-run of the search
$ snatcher search grab \-\-guid 'abc123' \-\-indexer 3

# ... or by the # the table showed, re-running the same query and filters
$ snatcher search grab 'ubuntu 24.04' 2 \-\-indexer 3

# write result 2's .torrent here instead, and add it to this server yourself
$ snatcher search download 'ubuntu 24.04' 2 \-o ubuntu.torrent
$ snatcher torrent add ubuntu.torrent

# raw REST call, for anything the typed commands do not cover
$ snatcher api \-\-prowlarr indexer \-\-jq '.[] | select(.enable) | .name'
.EE
.SS "Raw RPC calls"
.EX
# read the whole session, pick one field with jq
$ snatcher api session\-get \-\-jq '.arguments.version'

# call torrent-get with typed fields
$ snatcher api torrent\-get \-F ids:=1 \-F fields:='["id","name"]' \(rs
    \-\-jq '.arguments.torrents'
.EE
